<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phaistos Disc</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">2nd millennium BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Phaistos Disc is a clay disc found by the Italian archaeologist Luigi Pernier in the Minoan palace of Phaistos on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> in 1908. Both sides bear a spiral of 241 signs, of 45 different types, pressed into the clay with stamps, an early example of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/movable-type">movable type</a>. Its script and language are unknown and it has never been deciphered. It is kept in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Heraklion</a> Archaeological Museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phaistos-disc.jpg" alt="Phaistos Disc" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gleb Simonov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phaistos_Disc_%E2%80%94_Side_A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
